WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.759
There's nothing like a great TV finale: one
conclusive episode which gives a show's characters

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.680
and stories - not to mention the viewers who've
faithfully followed along - a truly satisfying

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.220
send-off.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.700
Unfortunately, however, there are plenty of
finales - often to really great shows - that

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.240
just can’t stick the landing.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.900
From controversial conclusions to all-out
storytelling disasters, these episodes rank

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.660
among the most frustrating TV finales ever
made.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.320
The Sopranos

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.380
A prestige HBO hit which heralded the dawn
of television’s golden age, The Sopranos

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:35.260
brought the medium to new heights, blurring
the long-standing lines between the small

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.460
screen and the large.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.120
After six seasons - and countless twists and
turns - only one question remained to round

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.220
off the show: what would be the fate of Tony
Soprano?

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.900
The finale’s final scene is one of TV’s
most notorious: Tony, whilst out at dinner,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.489
notices a man walk into the restaurant.

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:53.560
Is he just a fellow diner, or a hitman out
for blood?

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.460
Are any of the other patrons of the restaurant
out to get him?

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:00.870
The tension builds as his family arrives and
sits at the table one-by-one, but, just as

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.909
the scene appears to be moving towards a climax,
the screen abruptly cuts to black.

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:06.820
Tony's fate is left unresolved.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.440
The Sopranos’ ending is jarring to say the
least, and, while many appreciate its subversive

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.640
brilliance, it's hard to not empathize with
anybody who doesn’t.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:15.820
Dexter

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.079
Showtime’s flagship crime drama had already
fallen pretty far off the rails by the time

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:22.149
it came to an end.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:26.350
At this point, the show had wandered into
a bizarre final storyline and managed to botch

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:31.810
the only good thread the show had left to
follow - Dexter's terrible secret being discovered.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.869
Instead of any kind of definitive ending for
the character, Dexter fakes his own death

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:39.399
and escapes to Oregon, where he begins working
as a truck driver.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.460
It was a bizarre, bitter, and inconclusive
letdown for the few remaining fans of a once-great

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:44.960
show.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.020
Seinfeld

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.459
A precursor to some of today’s most popular
comedies about gangs of horrible people, Seinfeld

00:01:51.459 --> 00:01:55.899
built its brand on an uncomfortable kind of
humor led by characters far more loathsome

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.099
than your typical protagonists.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.820
In the process, it became one of television’s
most iconic and subversive shows.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.160
The series finale, however, is notorious for
all the wrong reasons.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.600
Seinfeld’s final episode found Elaine, Jerry,
George, and Kramer on trial for laughing at

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.650
a man who was being held at gunpoint.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.690
During the trial, every person they've wronged
over the course of the series testifies against

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.060
them, putting into perspective just how odious
each character is.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.100
By the end of the episode, they're all in
jail.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:27.360
A popular series ending with a glorified clip
show was a highly unusual move, and the finale

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.610
was deeply divisive amongst fans.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.030
Even Seinfeld himself admitted,

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.560
"I sometimes think we really shouldn't have
even done it."

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.460
“Very good, very good.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:36.380
You know something?

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.620
No soup for you!

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:39.740
Come back, one year!

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.020
Next!"

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.660
How I Met Your Mother

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.590
Kind of spells it all out in the title, doesn't
it?

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.740
No matter how long the show ran, how many
detours it took or how many characters it

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.980
followed, it was always going to be the tale
of how Ted, the protagonist, met the mother

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:55.800
of his children.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.960
As long as it answered that question in a
gripping and believable fashion, viewers were

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.080
going to be left happy.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.200
Spoiler alert, though: they weren’t.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:08.300
Rather than take the satisfying route, the
show’s writers decided to tack on a last-minute

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.680
stinger, explaining that the mother passed
away shortly before Ted started telling the

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.710
story to his kids, who insist that, the whole
time, he's actually been in love with his

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:16.500
friend Robin.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.020
“No, this is a story of how you're totally
in love with Aunt Robin."

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.040
This was a romantic pairing the show had attempted
earlier, before Robin moved on and married

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.840
Neil Patrick Harris' character Barney.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.980
To make matters worse, the show’s creators
actually filmed a happier ending, minus the

00:03:31.981 --> 00:03:35.510
whole death thing, and for some reason decided
to go with this one instead.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.320
It could have been so good.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:38.500
True Blood

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.120
When the entire premise of your show revolves
around a single, major choice a character

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.800
has to make, you’d better make sure you
wrap things up in a satisfying manner.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.600
To say True Blood failed to do this is an
understatement.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.160
Anna Paquin's Sookie Stackhouse - the small-town
waitress who finds herself wrapped up in the

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.910
world of the supernatural - spends the whole
series trying to decide where her heart truly

00:03:58.910 --> 00:03:59.400
lies.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.720
Is it with Bill, the vampiric southern gentleman?

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.380
Or with Eric, the icy Nordic vampire lord?

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.320
Or maybe she'll end up with Alcide, the jacked
werewolf construction worker.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.190
Sookie, as it turned out, ended up with none
of the above.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.150
Alcide and Bill are both dead by the time
the show has come to an end, while Eric has

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.269
just moved on.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:19.159
Instead, she ends up with... some human dude.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.060
Viewers, predictably, weren’t happy.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.120
Can you blame them?

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:23.640
Penny Dreadful

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.550
Penny Dreadful’s finale was, in many ways,
actually pretty great.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.979
It wrapped up every character arc and convincingly
resolved most of the show’s plotlines - and

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.000
with such a large ensemble cast, it was no
easy task.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.320
So why was this episode so frustrating?

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.260
Because nobody knew it was a finale.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.550
Some controversy exists as to just why Penny
Dreadful ended when it did, but the important

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:47.770
thing to know here is that the show’s third
season was never marketed as its last.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.699
It was only after the final shot had faded
out and the words “The End” appeared on

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:55.499
the screen that viewers realized they’d
just seen their last episode, ever.

00:04:55.499 --> 00:05:00.259
For the show’s many die-hard fans and followers,
it was a painful and sudden demise - which,

00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:03.849
considering the show in question, does seem
like a pretty apt way to end things.

